  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full text is present. It contains formatting artifacts and placeholder ACM citation metadata, but the study text is readable.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates downstream software-design task scores across model sizes, providing results relevant to whether downstream performance changes with scale. It does not measure pretraining loss or fit a scaling law.",
    "gate_note": "The relevant results are the study's own task scores across the three model sizes in Table 1. Mentions of neural scaling laws and findings from other studies are background or motivation, not results of this study.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares its own downstream task scores (Java cohesion and coupling analysis) across three DeepSeek-R1 distilled models of 14B, 32B and 70B, and explicitly reads the result as an effect of scale. That bears weakly on V6 in a new setting. Three points cannot show predictability, there is no loss or curve fit, and the 70B model switches backbone (Llama-3.3 vs Qwen2.5), so size is not isolated. This is a T3 setting result, not a T2 scaling test.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude the models have solid foundational knowledge of cohesion and coupling but brittle application under noise and low guidance. They also state that performance improves with size in nearly every condition, calling it an emergent property of scale, and cite neural scaling laws to expect the trends to generalize. They fit no scaling law.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is a generated-code benchmark of software-design task performance across three model variants, not a pretraining loss sweep or a scaling-law fit.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Task formulation and distortion affect reported task performance. The baseline size comparison also spans different model backbones: the 14B and 32B variants use Qwen2.5, while the 70B variant uses Llama-3.3-Instruct.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The model-size comparison has only three variants and changes backbone between the 70B model and the two smaller models, so size is not isolated. The paper reports no uncertainty estimates for the scores. Its scores concern generated Java design tasks, not held-out pretraining loss or a fitted predictive scaling curve.",
    "rows_left_out": "No additional Table 1 model-size results relevant to V6 were left out. Distortion-sweep and guidance results were not extracted as separate rows because they do not report a model-size scaling comparison.",
